WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.270
- [Narrator] Syrian
president Bashar al-Assad

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.190
is being welcomed back into the fold

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.890
of some Arab nations
after having been shunned

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.383
by most world leaders
for more than a decade.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.570
His brutal crackdown on
anti-government protests in 2011

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.583
triggered a civil war
that's still going on.

00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:19.710
(gun firing)

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.300
The conflicts led to sanctions

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.710
against the Syrian government

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.710
by its neighbors and western nations.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:29.490
But in recent weeks, fueled
by a devastating earthquake,

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.370
Assad has been rebuilding diplomatic ties

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.697
and asking for economic reprieve.

00:00:34.697 --> 00:00:38.010
From walking the red carpets in Oman

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.560
to meeting Egypt's foreign minister.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.880
Here's how Assad has shifted his image

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.093
and what it means for the Middle East.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.910
A devastating earthquake hits
Turkey and Syria in February.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.370
While most of the
casualties were in Turkey,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.220
more than 6,000 died in Syria.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.100
Assad's government said
Western sanctions from the war

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.900
hampered its rescue efforts.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.150
- Within hours of the
earthquake taking place

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.110
there was a meeting

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.020
of top level Syrian officials in Damascus.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.650
And the idea was now we're gonna
leverage this at two level.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.470
The first one, lobby against sanctions

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.200
for the end of sanctions,
not just exemptions.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.640
And two, we're gonna use it

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.780
as a tool to sort of
get our Arab neighbors

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.800
or other Arab countries
to get closer to us.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.880
- [Narrator] The earthquake appears

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.560
to have given Assad momentum

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.890
as he seeks to capitalize
on the humanitarian disaster

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.380
to reduce his isolation.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.170
In the days following the
disaster representative

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.020
of some countries, including
the United Arab Emirates,

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.540
Egypt, and Jordan visited Syria.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.420
- So really, you could see
that what was already ongoing

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.330
as a repressional sort of
accelerated at that stage.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:51.030
- [Narrator] Assad also made
only his second state visit

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.250
since 2011 to Oman,

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.430
a country that never
broke ties with Damascus.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.580
European and Arab officials
said Syria's president

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.300
used the trip to lobby for the
removal of Western sanctions

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.920
in return for keeping crossings

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.533
into rebel held areas in Syria open.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.040
Assad's effort to restore
ties have been building

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.640
since The Arab League,
a group of 22 nations,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.444
suspended Syria when
war broke out in 2011.

00:02:18.444 --> 00:02:22.111
(speaking foreign language)

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.260
- [Narrator] In the past few years,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.420
the Syrian leader has beat back rebels

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.820
with the help of Russia and Iran.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.925
Both countries remain key
partners to the regime.

00:02:35.925 --> 00:02:38.520
Assad arrived in Moscow in March

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.340
to hold talks with
President Vladimir Putin

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.730
to develop cooperation
between the two countries.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.070
And Syria has been
working with its neighbors

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.247
to curb the rise of terrorist groups

00:02:49.247 --> 00:02:52.143
and the trade in the
illicit drug Captagon.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.870
- For a number of countries in the region,

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.880
there were already some
kind of conversation

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.237
on counterterrorism, obviously
because of the presence

00:02:59.237 --> 00:03:01.664
of the Islamic State and other groups

00:03:01.664 --> 00:03:04.410
affiliated or close to Al-Qaeda.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.010
So there was already some very
sort of discreet engagement.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:09.960
That accelerated because of Captagon,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.150
because of this illicit
drug that started really

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.850
to flood the region from
Syria into countries

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:19.530
like Egypt, Saudi Arabia,
the UAE, and Jordan.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.360
- [Narrator] Now, a deal is being brokered

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.870
between Assad and some Arab
nations, including Jordan,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.050
to help Syria more formally reintegrate.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.260
It could benefit the region
for a number of reasons.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.910
- The prime concept is really stability.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:35.070
If you have a country in the region

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.650
that is where there's no rule of law

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.490
and everything is sort of collapsed

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.080
like we had at times
in Somalia or in Libya,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.810
it has implications for
the rest of of the region

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:46.920
and that's what they want to avoid.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.840
I think they want to avoid another Libya.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.970
- [Narrator] Somalia's central
government has struggled

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.030
for more than a decade to
contain terrorist attacks

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:57.120
from the Al-Qaeda linked group Al-Shabaab.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.620
Libya has been in turmoil

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.720
since longtime leader Muammar Gaddafi

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.793
was overthrown in 2011.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.530
For Syria, all of Assad's
diplomatic efforts come

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.040
against the backdrop of the
long drawn out civil war

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.800
which has spanned over 12 years.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.800
Hundreds of thousands of people have died

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.200
and the conflict's created
the world's largest

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.350
refugee crisis according
to the United Nations.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.090
But even as the war continues

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.150
and Arab countries bring
Assad back into the fold,

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:31.140
convincing the West to ease
tensions will be more difficult.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.660
- Their sanctions program,
which are not ready, again,

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.880
under this scenario to end

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.510
would be potentially an
impediment for Arab reproachment.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.790
- [Narrator] While the
UN said the status quo

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.910
of isolating Syria cannot continue.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.760
- There needs to be a
coordinated international effort

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.290
in support of this

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:53.493
with all key players working
in a coherent effort.

